1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,799
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:01,979 --> 00:00:04,410
The most dangerous money mistakes don't look

3
00:00:04,410 --> 00:00:05,183
like mistakes.

4
00:00:05,363 --> 00:00:08,405
Nobody brags about gambling away their savings.

5
00:00:08,585 --> 00:00:10,772
But people proudly tell you about the

6
00:00:10,772 --> 00:00:12,664
big house they got approved for,

7
00:00:12,664 --> 00:00:14,378
the degree they borrowed for,

8
00:00:14,378 --> 00:00:16,152
or the loan they co signed for

9
00:00:16,152 --> 00:00:16,979
their brother.

10
00:00:17,159 --> 00:00:20,935
Today, fifteen financial traps disguised as good

11
00:00:20,935 --> 00:00:21,722
decisions.

12
00:00:22,072 --> 00:00:23,558
Fifteen traps.

13
00:00:23,738 --> 00:00:25,577
Each one looks responsible,

14
00:00:25,577 --> 00:00:27,824
generous or smart on the surface.

15
00:00:28,004 --> 00:00:30,613
Each one can quietly cost you thousands,

16
00:00:30,613 --> 00:00:32,439
or even decades of progress.

17
00:00:32,790 --> 00:00:35,030
Here's why these traps are so effective.

18
00:00:35,210 --> 00:00:37,601
A bad decision that looks bad is

19
00:00:37,601 --> 00:00:38,647
easy to avoid.

20
00:00:38,827 --> 00:00:40,216
Your friends warn you,

21
00:00:40,216 --> 00:00:41,416
your gut warns you.

22
00:00:41,596 --> 00:00:43,587
But a bad decision that looks good

23
00:00:43,587 --> 00:00:44,289
gets praise.

24
00:00:44,469 --> 00:00:46,292
Your parents congratulate you.

25
00:00:46,472 --> 00:00:48,515
The salesperson calls you smart.

26
00:00:48,695 --> 00:00:50,158
Society rewards it.

27
00:00:50,338 --> 00:00:52,198
So you walk in with a smile,

28
00:00:52,198 --> 00:00:54,789
and only discover the cost years later,

29
00:00:54,789 --> 00:00:56,317
when it's hard to undo.

30
00:00:56,668 --> 00:00:59,029
We'll look at these traps from three

31
00:00:59,029 --> 00:00:59,489
angles.

32
00:00:59,669 --> 00:01:01,198
Angle one, the numbers:

33
00:01:01,198 --> 00:01:03,657
traps where the math is hidden behind

34
00:01:03,657 --> 00:01:04,522
a good story.

35
00:01:04,702 --> 00:01:06,850
Angle two, the psychology:

36
00:01:06,850 --> 00:01:08,503
traps that use love,

37
00:01:08,503 --> 00:01:11,064
pride and identity against you.

38
00:01:11,244 --> 00:01:12,294
And angle three,

39
00:01:12,294 --> 00:01:14,721
the playbook: the traps that hit your

40
00:01:14,721 --> 00:01:15,837
long term wealth,

41
00:01:15,837 --> 00:01:17,936
and a simple filter to catch any

42
00:01:17,936 --> 00:01:19,708
trap before you step in it.

43
00:01:20,058 --> 00:01:22,699
Number thirteen is one that thousands of

44
00:01:22,699 --> 00:01:25,275
people fell into at one famous company,

45
00:01:25,275 --> 00:01:27,058
and lost almost everything.

46
00:01:27,238 --> 00:01:28,794
Let's start with the numbers.

47
00:01:29,144 --> 00:01:31,156
Every number here comes from a named

48
00:01:31,156 --> 00:01:31,547
source.

49
00:01:31,727 --> 00:01:34,204
Student loan totals come from the Federal

50
00:01:34,204 --> 00:01:34,687
Reserve.

51
00:01:34,867 --> 00:01:37,886
Retirement account rules come from the IRS.

52
00:01:38,066 --> 00:01:40,406
Co signing rules come from the Federal

53
00:01:40,406 --> 00:01:43,116
Trade Commission's required cosigner notice.

54
00:01:43,296 --> 00:01:44,813
Examples use stated,

55
00:01:44,813 --> 00:01:47,921
simple math, like a seven percent average

56
00:01:47,921 --> 00:01:50,954
yearly return, which is an illustration,

57
00:01:50,954 --> 00:01:52,015
not a promise.

58
00:01:52,195 --> 00:01:54,359
Your situation may be different,

59
00:01:54,359 --> 00:01:56,185
so treat this as education,

60
00:01:56,185 --> 00:01:57,606
not financial advice.

61
00:01:57,956 --> 00:01:58,745
Angle one.

62
00:01:58,925 --> 00:02:00,974
Traps where the math hides behind a

63
00:02:00,974 --> 00:02:01,619
good story.

64
00:02:01,969 --> 00:02:03,199
Trap number one.

65
00:02:03,379 --> 00:02:05,355
Buying the biggest house the bank will

66
00:02:05,355 --> 00:02:05,771
approve.

67
00:02:06,121 --> 00:02:08,700
Getting approved for a big mortgage feels

68
00:02:08,700 --> 00:02:10,335
like a vote of confidence.

69
00:02:10,515 --> 00:02:12,531
The bank looked at your numbers and

70
00:02:12,531 --> 00:02:14,546
said yes, so it must be affordable,

71
00:02:14,546 --> 00:02:14,892
right?

72
00:02:15,072 --> 00:02:16,303
Not necessarily.

73
00:02:16,483 --> 00:02:19,216
Lenders approve based on rules about your

74
00:02:19,216 --> 00:02:20,349
income and debts.

75
00:02:20,529 --> 00:02:22,744
They don't know about your plans to

76
00:02:22,744 --> 00:02:24,390
have kids, change careers,

77
00:02:24,390 --> 00:02:25,846
travel or retire early.

78
00:02:26,026 --> 00:02:28,476
Approval tells you the maximum risk the

79
00:02:28,476 --> 00:02:29,544
lender will take.

80
00:02:29,724 --> 00:02:31,303
Not the right amount for your life.

81
00:02:31,653 --> 00:02:34,056
A bigger house brings bigger everything.

82
00:02:34,236 --> 00:02:35,908
Higher property taxes,

83
00:02:35,908 --> 00:02:38,568
higher insurance, higher utilities,

84
00:02:38,568 --> 00:02:40,772
more furniture, more repairs.

85
00:02:40,952 --> 00:02:43,158
Many people who max out their approval

86
00:02:43,158 --> 00:02:44,203
end up house poor.

87
00:02:44,383 --> 00:02:46,074
They own a beautiful home,

88
00:02:46,074 --> 00:02:47,375
but they can't save,

89
00:02:47,375 --> 00:02:49,976
can't invest, and live one surprise away

90
00:02:49,976 --> 00:02:50,757
from stress.

91
00:02:50,937 --> 00:02:52,627
The house was supposed to be a

92
00:02:52,627 --> 00:02:53,247
foundation.

93
00:02:53,427 --> 00:02:54,751
It became a ceiling.

94
00:02:55,101 --> 00:02:57,526
A common guideline is to keep total

95
00:02:57,526 --> 00:03:00,228
housing costs around a quarter to under

96
00:03:00,228 --> 00:03:02,237
a third of your gross income,

97
00:03:02,237 --> 00:03:04,316
including taxes and insurance,

98
00:03:04,316 --> 00:03:05,840
not just the mortgage.

99
00:03:06,020 --> 00:03:08,254
Try this: when you get your approval

100
00:03:08,254 --> 00:03:10,489
amount, decide in advance to shop at

101
00:03:10,489 --> 00:03:12,475
seventy to eighty percent of it.

102
00:03:12,655 --> 00:03:14,497
The extra room in your budget will

103
00:03:14,497 --> 00:03:16,285
be worth more than the extra room

104
00:03:16,285 --> 00:03:17,044
in your house.

105
00:03:17,394 --> 00:03:18,311
Already in?

106
00:03:18,491 --> 00:03:20,579
If your house feels too big for

107
00:03:20,579 --> 00:03:22,601
your budget, you have options.

108
00:03:22,781 --> 00:03:25,161
Look into refinancing if rates drop.

109
00:03:25,341 --> 00:03:27,922
Appeal your property tax assessment if it

110
00:03:27,922 --> 00:03:28,615
seems high.

111
00:03:28,795 --> 00:03:31,140
Shop your home insurance every year.

112
00:03:31,320 --> 00:03:34,054
Consider renting a room or a basement

113
00:03:34,054 --> 00:03:36,196
unit if local rules allow it.

114
00:03:36,376 --> 00:03:38,068
And if none of that works,

115
00:03:38,068 --> 00:03:40,475
selling and downsizing isn't failure.

116
00:03:40,655 --> 00:03:42,410
It's a reset that can free up

117
00:03:42,410 --> 00:03:43,499
years of progress.

118
00:03:43,849 --> 00:03:45,126
Trap number two.

119
00:03:45,306 --> 00:03:47,190
Rushing to pay off a low rate

120
00:03:47,190 --> 00:03:49,985
mortgage while skipping retirement savings.

121
00:03:50,335 --> 00:03:52,613
Being debt free is a wonderful goal,

122
00:03:52,613 --> 00:03:54,892
and paying extra on a mortgage feels

123
00:03:54,892 --> 00:03:55,652
responsible.

124
00:03:55,832 --> 00:03:57,086
But the order matters.

125
00:03:57,266 --> 00:03:59,068
If your mortgage rate is low,

126
00:03:59,068 --> 00:04:00,685
say three or four percent,

127
00:04:00,685 --> 00:04:03,731
and you're not getting your employer's retirement

128
00:04:03,731 --> 00:04:05,720
match, you may be leaving a much

129
00:04:05,720 --> 00:04:07,274
better deal on the table.

130
00:04:07,454 --> 00:04:09,244
A match can be like an instant

131
00:04:09,244 --> 00:04:11,511
fifty or one hundred percent return on

132
00:04:11,511 --> 00:04:12,585
the matched money.

133
00:04:12,936 --> 00:04:15,112
Money sent to a mortgage is also

134
00:04:15,112 --> 00:04:16,744
locked inside the house.

135
00:04:16,924 --> 00:04:18,133
If you lose your job,

136
00:04:18,133 --> 00:04:20,147
the bank doesn't care that you paid

137
00:04:20,147 --> 00:04:21,068
extra last year.

138
00:04:21,248 --> 00:04:22,862
You still owe the next payment.

139
00:04:23,042 --> 00:04:26,017
Money in an emergency fund or investments

140
00:04:26,017 --> 00:04:27,106
stays flexible.

141
00:04:27,286 --> 00:04:29,290
Some people end up with lots of

142
00:04:29,290 --> 00:04:31,423
home equity and very little cash,

143
00:04:31,423 --> 00:04:33,427
which is a fragile place to be.

144
00:04:33,778 --> 00:04:35,542
This doesn't mean never pay extra.

145
00:04:35,722 --> 00:04:37,028
If your rate is high,

146
00:04:37,028 --> 00:04:39,391
or you're close to retirement and want

147
00:04:39,391 --> 00:04:41,444
lower expenses, it can make a lot

148
00:04:41,444 --> 00:04:42,003
of sense.

149
00:04:42,183 --> 00:04:44,807
Try this: before paying extra on your

150
00:04:44,807 --> 00:04:46,792
mortgage, check three boxes.

151
00:04:46,972 --> 00:04:48,504
Full employer match,

152
00:04:48,504 --> 00:04:50,113
solid emergency fund,

153
00:04:50,113 --> 00:04:52,104
and no high interest debt.

154
00:04:52,284 --> 00:04:53,143
Then decide.

155
00:04:53,494 --> 00:04:54,364
Already in?

156
00:04:54,544 --> 00:04:56,458
If you've been paying extra on a

157
00:04:56,458 --> 00:04:59,090
low rate mortgage while skipping your match,

158
00:04:59,090 --> 00:05:00,047
the fix is easy.

159
00:05:00,227 --> 00:05:03,003
Redirect the extra payment to your retirement

160
00:05:03,003 --> 00:05:05,100
plan until you get the full match,

161
00:05:05,100 --> 00:05:06,950
and build your emergency fund.

162
00:05:07,130 --> 00:05:09,835
The equity you already built isn't lost.

163
00:05:10,015 --> 00:05:12,069
You're just changing where the next dollar

164
00:05:12,069 --> 00:05:12,314
goes.

165
00:05:12,664 --> 00:05:14,010
Trap number three.

166
00:05:14,190 --> 00:05:16,270
Keeping all your money in a savings

167
00:05:16,270 --> 00:05:17,221
account forever.

168
00:05:17,571 --> 00:05:18,975
Saving is great.

169
00:05:19,155 --> 00:05:21,988
Keeping everything in savings forever is a

170
00:05:21,988 --> 00:05:22,325
trap.

171
00:05:22,505 --> 00:05:25,249
A savings account protects you from market

172
00:05:25,249 --> 00:05:28,058
drops, which is perfect for emergencies and

173
00:05:28,058 --> 00:05:29,169
short term goals.

174
00:05:29,349 --> 00:05:30,592
But over decades,

175
00:05:30,592 --> 00:05:32,565
inflation slowly eats cash.

176
00:05:32,745 --> 00:05:34,986
If prices rise three percent a year

177
00:05:34,986 --> 00:05:37,164
and your savings earn one percent,

178
00:05:37,164 --> 00:05:39,789
you're losing about two percent of buying

179
00:05:39,789 --> 00:05:40,878
power every year,

180
00:05:40,878 --> 00:05:42,799
while feeling completely safe.

181
00:05:43,150 --> 00:05:45,689
Picture fifty thousand dollars sitting in a

182
00:05:45,689 --> 00:05:47,934
low interest account for twenty years,

183
00:05:47,934 --> 00:05:49,942
losing about two percent a year in

184
00:05:49,942 --> 00:05:50,592
real terms.

185
00:05:50,772 --> 00:05:52,832
Its buying power could fall to roughly

186
00:05:52,832 --> 00:05:55,381
thirty three thousand dollars in today's money.

187
00:05:55,561 --> 00:05:58,070
The same money invested in a diversified

188
00:05:58,070 --> 00:06:00,829
portfolio has historically had a good chance

189
00:06:00,829 --> 00:06:03,276
of growing much more over twenty years,

190
00:06:03,276 --> 00:06:05,596
although with ups and downs along the

191
00:06:05,596 --> 00:06:05,847
way.

192
00:06:06,198 --> 00:06:08,660
Try this: give every dollar a time

193
00:06:08,660 --> 00:06:09,239
horizon.

194
00:06:09,419 --> 00:06:11,272
Money you need within a few years

195
00:06:11,272 --> 00:06:12,508
stays safe and liquid.

196
00:06:12,688 --> 00:06:14,765
Money for ten or twenty years from

197
00:06:14,765 --> 00:06:16,658
now can be invested for growth.

198
00:06:16,838 --> 00:06:18,290
The trap isn't saving.

199
00:06:18,470 --> 00:06:20,780
It's using one tool for every job.

200
00:06:21,130 --> 00:06:21,861
Already in?

201
00:06:22,041 --> 00:06:23,997
If most of your long term money

202
00:06:23,997 --> 00:06:26,332
is sitting in a low interest account,

203
00:06:26,332 --> 00:06:28,730
start by moving your emergency fund to

204
00:06:28,730 --> 00:06:30,560
a high yield savings account,

205
00:06:30,560 --> 00:06:32,769
which pays more while staying safe.

206
00:06:32,949 --> 00:06:35,029
Then, for money you won't need for

207
00:06:35,029 --> 00:06:37,538
many years, consider investing gradually,

208
00:06:37,538 --> 00:06:39,007
a set amount each month,

209
00:06:39,007 --> 00:06:40,965
so you don't have to worry about

210
00:06:40,965 --> 00:06:42,434
picking the perfect day.

211
00:06:42,784 --> 00:06:44,200
Trap number four.

212
00:06:44,380 --> 00:06:46,470
Buying something because it's on sale.

213
00:06:46,820 --> 00:06:49,780
Sales trigger something powerful in our brains.

214
00:06:49,960 --> 00:06:52,468
Fifty percent off feels like making money.

215
00:06:52,648 --> 00:06:54,310
But if you didn't plan to buy

216
00:06:54,310 --> 00:06:56,259
it, you didn't save fifty percent.

217
00:06:56,439 --> 00:06:58,029
You spent fifty percent.

218
00:06:58,209 --> 00:07:00,206
Retailers know this well.

219
00:07:00,386 --> 00:07:01,635
Limited time offers,

220
00:07:01,635 --> 00:07:05,070
countdown timers, and strikethrough prices are designed

221
00:07:05,070 --> 00:07:07,132
to make you act before you think.

222
00:07:07,482 --> 00:07:08,804
Many reference prices,

223
00:07:08,804 --> 00:07:10,969
the original price shown next to the

224
00:07:10,969 --> 00:07:13,193
sale price, are higher than what most

225
00:07:13,193 --> 00:07:14,215
people ever paid.

226
00:07:14,395 --> 00:07:16,813
And seasonal sales create a sense of

227
00:07:16,813 --> 00:07:18,424
urgency every few weeks.

228
00:07:18,604 --> 00:07:21,059
Black Friday, then holiday sales,

229
00:07:21,059 --> 00:07:22,547
then New Year sales,

230
00:07:22,547 --> 00:07:23,887
then spring sales.

231
00:07:24,067 --> 00:07:24,818
If you wait,

232
00:07:24,818 --> 00:07:26,946
there's almost always another one.

233
00:07:27,296 --> 00:07:29,693
Try this: before any sale purchase,

234
00:07:29,693 --> 00:07:31,680
ask, would I buy this at full

235
00:07:31,680 --> 00:07:32,091
price?

236
00:07:32,271 --> 00:07:33,363
If the answer is no,

237
00:07:33,363 --> 00:07:35,219
you probably don't really need it.

238
00:07:35,399 --> 00:07:37,210
Keep a wish list of things you

239
00:07:37,210 --> 00:07:39,443
actually want, and only buy from that

240
00:07:39,443 --> 00:07:41,495
list when a real discount appears.

241
00:07:41,675 --> 00:07:43,378
That's how sales work for you,

242
00:07:43,378 --> 00:07:45,251
instead of you working for sales.

243
00:07:45,601 --> 00:07:46,495
Already in?

244
00:07:46,675 --> 00:07:48,532
Look at the last three months of

245
00:07:48,532 --> 00:07:51,261
purchases and circle anything you bought mainly

246
00:07:51,261 --> 00:07:52,770
because it was discounted.

247
00:07:52,950 --> 00:07:53,496
Add it up.

248
00:07:53,676 --> 00:07:55,138
That's your sale tax.

249
00:07:55,318 --> 00:07:58,179
Then unsubscribe from store emails and turn

250
00:07:58,179 --> 00:08:00,241
off shopping app notifications.

251
00:08:00,421 --> 00:08:03,068
Fewer reminders mean fewer impulses.

252
00:08:03,248 --> 00:08:05,880
Many people cut spending noticeably just by

253
00:08:05,880 --> 00:08:07,288
reducing what they see.

254
00:08:07,639 --> 00:08:08,997
Trap number five.

255
00:08:09,177 --> 00:08:11,708
Extended warranties on everyday things.

256
00:08:12,058 --> 00:08:12,855
At the checkout,

257
00:08:12,855 --> 00:08:14,449
you're often offered protection.

258
00:08:14,629 --> 00:08:17,206
Two more years of coverage on your

259
00:08:17,206 --> 00:08:19,783
TV, laptop or blender for a modest

260
00:08:19,783 --> 00:08:20,086
fee.

261
00:08:20,266 --> 00:08:21,858
It feels responsible,

262
00:08:21,858 --> 00:08:22,994
like insurance.

263
00:08:23,174 --> 00:08:25,662
But many products don't fail during the

264
00:08:25,662 --> 00:08:28,596
extended period, and many failures are already

265
00:08:28,596 --> 00:08:31,403
covered by the manufacturer's warranty or by

266
00:08:31,403 --> 00:08:33,890
your credit card's purchase protection.

267
00:08:34,241 --> 00:08:35,151
For the store,

268
00:08:35,151 --> 00:08:37,624
these plans are often very profitable,

269
00:08:37,624 --> 00:08:39,900
because the price is set well above

270
00:08:39,900 --> 00:08:41,787
the expected cost of repairs.

271
00:08:41,967 --> 00:08:43,105
That's not a scam.

272
00:08:43,285 --> 00:08:45,404
It's just math that's designed to favor

273
00:08:45,404 --> 00:08:46,002
the seller.

274
00:08:46,182 --> 00:08:48,380
Insurance makes the most sense for big

275
00:08:48,380 --> 00:08:50,926
risks you couldn't afford to cover yourself,

276
00:08:50,926 --> 00:08:51,910
like your health,

277
00:08:51,910 --> 00:08:53,357
your home or your income.

278
00:08:53,537 --> 00:08:55,266
Not a sixty dollar toaster.

279
00:08:55,617 --> 00:08:57,823
Try this: self insure.

280
00:08:58,003 --> 00:08:59,636
Skip most small warranties,

281
00:08:59,636 --> 00:09:01,572
and put what you would have paid

282
00:09:01,572 --> 00:09:03,811
into a savings bucket for repairs and

283
00:09:03,811 --> 00:09:04,597
replacements.

284
00:09:04,777 --> 00:09:07,314
Over time, many people find the bucket

285
00:09:07,314 --> 00:09:09,517
covers their occasional failures,

286
00:09:09,517 --> 00:09:10,919
with money left over.

287
00:09:11,269 --> 00:09:12,093
Already in?

288
00:09:12,273 --> 00:09:15,458
Many extended warranties can be canceled for

289
00:09:15,458 --> 00:09:16,761
a prorated refund,

290
00:09:16,761 --> 00:09:19,657
especially in the first weeks or months.

291
00:09:19,837 --> 00:09:20,940
Check your contract.

292
00:09:21,120 --> 00:09:23,268
And check your credit card benefits too.

293
00:09:23,448 --> 00:09:26,563
Some cards extend the manufacturer's warranty for

294
00:09:26,563 --> 00:09:28,916
free on purchases made with the card.

295
00:09:29,096 --> 00:09:31,262
You might already have coverage you're paying

296
00:09:31,262 --> 00:09:31,743
for twice.

297
00:09:32,093 --> 00:09:33,497
Trap number six.

298
00:09:33,677 --> 00:09:36,058
Celebrating a big tax refund.

299
00:09:36,408 --> 00:09:39,071
We covered this briefly in another video,

300
00:09:39,071 --> 00:09:40,695
but it's worth repeating,

301
00:09:40,695 --> 00:09:42,189
because it's so common.

302
00:09:42,369 --> 00:09:45,109
A big tax refund feels like winning.

303
00:09:45,289 --> 00:09:47,580
In reality, it means you overpaid your

304
00:09:47,580 --> 00:09:49,147
taxes throughout the year,

305
00:09:49,147 --> 00:09:51,679
and the government held your money without

306
00:09:51,679 --> 00:09:53,126
paying you any interest.

307
00:09:53,476 --> 00:09:55,741
If you're carrying credit card debt while

308
00:09:55,741 --> 00:09:56,901
waiting for a refund,

309
00:09:56,901 --> 00:09:58,062
it can be even worse.

310
00:09:58,242 --> 00:10:00,569
You're paying twenty percent on the card

311
00:10:00,569 --> 00:10:03,012
while lending the government money at zero

312
00:10:03,012 --> 00:10:03,478
percent.

313
00:10:03,658 --> 00:10:06,061
Adjusting your withholding could give you that

314
00:10:06,061 --> 00:10:07,158
money every paycheck,

315
00:10:07,158 --> 00:10:08,778
which you could use to pay down

316
00:10:08,778 --> 00:10:10,032
the card months earlier.

317
00:10:10,382 --> 00:10:13,343
Try this: use the IRS withholding estimator

318
00:10:13,343 --> 00:10:15,685
on the official IRS website once a

319
00:10:15,685 --> 00:10:18,509
year, especially after a big life change.

320
00:10:18,689 --> 00:10:20,404
If a big refund helps you save

321
00:10:20,404 --> 00:10:22,690
because you'd spend the money otherwise,

322
00:10:22,690 --> 00:10:23,948
that's a valid choice.

323
00:10:24,128 --> 00:10:25,556
Just make it on purpose.

324
00:10:25,906 --> 00:10:26,858
Already in?

325
00:10:27,038 --> 00:10:29,168
This year, when the refund arrives,

326
00:10:29,168 --> 00:10:30,811
give it a job the same day.

327
00:10:30,991 --> 00:10:33,005
For example, most of it to high

328
00:10:33,005 --> 00:10:35,409
interest debt or your emergency fund,

329
00:10:35,409 --> 00:10:37,748
and a small slice for something fun.

330
00:10:37,928 --> 00:10:40,369
Next year, adjust your withholding so the

331
00:10:40,369 --> 00:10:42,692
money arrives in your paycheck instead,

332
00:10:42,692 --> 00:10:44,836
and automate it straight to savings.

333
00:10:45,186 --> 00:10:46,660
Six math traps.

334
00:10:46,840 --> 00:10:48,774
Now the ones that are harder to

335
00:10:48,774 --> 00:10:51,020
see, because they use your emotions.

336
00:10:51,370 --> 00:10:52,182
Angle two.

337
00:10:52,362 --> 00:10:53,888
Traps that use love,

338
00:10:53,888 --> 00:10:56,252
pride and identity against you.

339
00:10:56,602 --> 00:10:57,983
Trap number seven.

340
00:10:58,163 --> 00:11:00,137
Borrowing whatever it takes for a degree.

341
00:11:00,487 --> 00:11:02,396
Education can be one of the best

342
00:11:02,396 --> 00:11:03,888
investments in your life.

343
00:11:04,068 --> 00:11:07,171
On average, people with college degrees earn

344
00:11:07,171 --> 00:11:08,863
more over their careers.

345
00:11:09,043 --> 00:11:10,831
But on average hides a lot.

346
00:11:11,011 --> 00:11:12,341
The cost of the school,

347
00:11:12,341 --> 00:11:13,439
the field of study,

348
00:11:13,439 --> 00:11:14,537
whether you finish,

349
00:11:14,537 --> 00:11:16,561
and how much you borrow all matter.

350
00:11:16,741 --> 00:11:18,933
According to the Federal Reserve,

351
00:11:18,933 --> 00:11:21,390
Americans owe more than one point six

352
00:11:21,390 --> 00:11:23,649
trillion dollars in student loans.

353
00:11:23,999 --> 00:11:26,084
The trap is treating any degree,

354
00:11:26,084 --> 00:11:26,931
at any price,

355
00:11:26,931 --> 00:11:28,364
as automatically good.

356
00:11:28,544 --> 00:11:31,021
Borrowing a large amount for a program

357
00:11:31,021 --> 00:11:32,976
with modest expected earnings,

358
00:11:32,976 --> 00:11:35,127
or for an expensive school when a

359
00:11:35,127 --> 00:11:37,343
cheaper one offers a similar path,

360
00:11:37,343 --> 00:11:39,690
can lead to years of large payments.

361
00:11:39,870 --> 00:11:42,579
And student loans are generally very hard

362
00:11:42,579 --> 00:11:44,363
to discharge in bankruptcy.

363
00:11:44,713 --> 00:11:46,459
A common rule of thumb is to

364
00:11:46,459 --> 00:11:48,828
avoid borrowing more in total than you

365
00:11:48,828 --> 00:11:50,886
expect to earn in your first year

366
00:11:50,886 --> 00:11:51,946
after graduation.

367
00:11:52,126 --> 00:11:54,650
Try this: before choosing a school or

368
00:11:54,650 --> 00:11:57,516
program, look up typical earnings for that

369
00:11:57,516 --> 00:11:59,904
field and compare the total cost of

370
00:11:59,904 --> 00:12:00,996
several schools.

371
00:12:01,176 --> 00:12:05,345
The government's College Scorecard website shows costs

372
00:12:05,345 --> 00:12:08,664
and typical earnings by school and program.

373
00:12:09,014 --> 00:12:09,792
Already in?

374
00:12:09,972 --> 00:12:11,786
If student loans feel heavy,

375
00:12:11,786 --> 00:12:14,962
look into the federal repayment options available

376
00:12:14,962 --> 00:12:15,934
for your loans,

377
00:12:15,934 --> 00:12:17,878
including income driven plans,

378
00:12:17,878 --> 00:12:20,212
on the official student aid website.

379
00:12:20,392 --> 00:12:22,695
Avoid companies that charge fees for help

380
00:12:22,695 --> 00:12:23,875
you can get for free.

381
00:12:24,055 --> 00:12:26,602
And remember, your earnings are the biggest

382
00:12:26,602 --> 00:12:26,957
lever.

383
00:12:27,137 --> 00:12:29,698
Investing in skills that raise your income

384
00:12:29,698 --> 00:12:32,138
can shrink the loan faster than anything

385
00:12:32,138 --> 00:12:32,443
else.

386
00:12:32,793 --> 00:12:33,942
Trap number eight.

387
00:12:34,122 --> 00:12:36,290
Co signing a loan for someone you

388
00:12:36,290 --> 00:12:36,618
love.

389
00:12:36,968 --> 00:12:38,825
Co signing feels generous.

390
00:12:39,005 --> 00:12:41,795
Your child, sibling or friend can't qualify

391
00:12:41,795 --> 00:12:44,845
alone, and your signature makes the difference.

392
00:12:45,025 --> 00:12:47,318
But co signing doesn't mean vouching for

393
00:12:47,318 --> 00:12:47,777
someone.

394
00:12:47,957 --> 00:12:49,890
It means you are legally responsible for

395
00:12:49,890 --> 00:12:50,615
the whole debt.

396
00:12:50,795 --> 00:12:54,010
The Federal Trade Commission's cosigner notice warns

397
00:12:54,010 --> 00:12:55,741
that you may have to pay the

398
00:12:55,741 --> 00:12:57,966
full amount if the borrower doesn't,

399
00:12:57,966 --> 00:13:00,130
plus late fees or collection costs.

400
00:13:00,480 --> 00:13:02,116
And the loan shows up on your

401
00:13:02,116 --> 00:13:02,906
credit report.

402
00:13:03,086 --> 00:13:04,727
If the borrower pays late,

403
00:13:04,727 --> 00:13:05,989
your score can drop.

404
00:13:06,169 --> 00:13:08,005
The debt can also make it harder

405
00:13:08,005 --> 00:13:09,783
for you to qualify for your own

406
00:13:09,783 --> 00:13:10,128
loans.

407
00:13:10,308 --> 00:13:12,860
Lenders asked for a co signer because

408
00:13:12,860 --> 00:13:13,825
they saw risk.

409
00:13:14,005 --> 00:13:16,316
Your signature doesn't remove that risk.

410
00:13:16,496 --> 00:13:17,599
It moves it to you.

411
00:13:17,949 --> 00:13:20,743
Co signing can also strain relationships in

412
00:13:20,743 --> 00:13:22,302
a way money gifts don't.

413
00:13:22,482 --> 00:13:23,849
If the borrower struggles,

414
00:13:23,849 --> 00:13:25,478
you're suddenly their creditor.

415
00:13:25,658 --> 00:13:27,639
Try this: only co sign if you

416
00:13:27,639 --> 00:13:30,849
could comfortably pay the entire debt yourself,

417
00:13:30,849 --> 00:13:32,693
and would be okay doing so.

418
00:13:32,873 --> 00:13:35,255
If not, consider helping in a different

419
00:13:35,255 --> 00:13:37,269
way, like a smaller gift toward a

420
00:13:37,269 --> 00:13:38,063
down payment.

421
00:13:38,413 --> 00:13:39,179
Already in?

422
00:13:39,359 --> 00:13:41,381
Some loans allow a co signer to

423
00:13:41,381 --> 00:13:44,054
be released after the main borrower makes

424
00:13:44,054 --> 00:13:46,402
a certain number of on time payments

425
00:13:46,402 --> 00:13:47,706
and qualifies alone.

426
00:13:47,886 --> 00:13:48,792
Ask the lender.

427
00:13:48,972 --> 00:13:51,421
The borrower could also refinance the loan

428
00:13:51,421 --> 00:13:53,463
in their own name once their credit

429
00:13:53,463 --> 00:13:53,988
improves.

430
00:13:54,168 --> 00:13:57,035
Until then, monitor the account closely.

431
00:13:57,386 --> 00:13:58,802
Trap number nine.

432
00:13:58,982 --> 00:14:01,223
Lending money to friends and family.

433
00:14:01,573 --> 00:14:03,449
Helping people you love is a good

434
00:14:03,449 --> 00:14:03,790
thing.

435
00:14:03,970 --> 00:14:06,746
But personal loans between friends and family

436
00:14:06,746 --> 00:14:08,904
often go wrong in predictable ways.

437
00:14:09,084 --> 00:14:10,824
There's no clear schedule,

438
00:14:10,824 --> 00:14:13,031
no consequences for late payment,

439
00:14:13,031 --> 00:14:14,704
and a lot of awkwardness.

440
00:14:14,884 --> 00:14:17,240
The lender starts to resent the borrower.

441
00:14:17,420 --> 00:14:19,661
The borrower starts to avoid the lender.

442
00:14:19,841 --> 00:14:22,639
Both lose something more valuable than money.

443
00:14:22,989 --> 00:14:25,354
A useful approach many people follow:

444
00:14:25,354 --> 00:14:27,527
only lend what you'd be willing to

445
00:14:27,527 --> 00:14:28,167
give away.

446
00:14:28,347 --> 00:14:29,557
If it comes back,

447
00:14:29,557 --> 00:14:29,984
great.

448
00:14:30,164 --> 00:14:30,943
If it doesn't,

449
00:14:30,943 --> 00:14:33,561
you've decided in advance that the relationship

450
00:14:33,561 --> 00:14:34,285
matters more.

451
00:14:34,465 --> 00:14:37,114
This protects both your finances and your

452
00:14:37,114 --> 00:14:38,018
peace of mind.

453
00:14:38,368 --> 00:14:40,131
Try this: if you do lend,

454
00:14:40,131 --> 00:14:42,530
write down the amount and a simple

455
00:14:42,530 --> 00:14:44,858
repayment plan, and both sign it.

456
00:14:45,038 --> 00:14:46,338
It's not about distrust.

457
00:14:46,518 --> 00:14:47,714
It's about clarity.

458
00:14:47,894 --> 00:14:51,547
Clear expectations protect relationships far better than

459
00:14:51,547 --> 00:14:52,526
vague promises.

460
00:14:52,876 --> 00:14:53,561
Already in?

461
00:14:53,741 --> 00:14:55,756
If someone owes you money and it's

462
00:14:55,756 --> 00:14:57,237
hurting the relationship,

463
00:14:57,237 --> 00:14:58,837
have one calm conversation.

464
00:14:59,017 --> 00:15:00,321
Agree on a small,

465
00:15:00,321 --> 00:15:02,084
realistic payment plan,

466
00:15:02,084 --> 00:15:04,613
or decide to forgive the loan and

467
00:15:04,613 --> 00:15:05,380
let it go.

468
00:15:05,560 --> 00:15:08,323
Either choice is better than silent resentment.

469
00:15:08,503 --> 00:15:09,483
And next time,

470
00:15:09,483 --> 00:15:12,075
decide in advance whether it's a loan

471
00:15:12,075 --> 00:15:12,775
or a gift.

472
00:15:13,126 --> 00:15:14,472
Trap number ten.

473
00:15:14,652 --> 00:15:15,837
Buying a timeshare.

474
00:15:16,187 --> 00:15:19,101
Timeshare presentations are famously persuasive.

475
00:15:19,281 --> 00:15:21,164
Free gifts, a beautiful resort,

476
00:15:21,164 --> 00:15:23,108
a sales team trained to make you

477
00:15:23,108 --> 00:15:25,538
feel like you're investing in a lifetime

478
00:15:25,538 --> 00:15:26,328
of vacations.

479
00:15:26,508 --> 00:15:29,482
But many timeshares come with yearly maintenance

480
00:15:29,482 --> 00:15:31,526
fees that tend to rise over time,

481
00:15:31,526 --> 00:15:33,509
whether you use the week or not.

482
00:15:33,859 --> 00:15:36,099
And the resale market is often weak.

483
00:15:36,279 --> 00:15:38,681
Many owners find their timeshare is worth

484
00:15:38,681 --> 00:15:40,732
a small fraction of what they paid,

485
00:15:40,732 --> 00:15:42,665
and some struggle to sell at all.

486
00:15:42,845 --> 00:15:45,236
A purchase that felt like an asset

487
00:15:45,236 --> 00:15:47,768
can turn into an ongoing obligation.

488
00:15:47,948 --> 00:15:50,540
The Federal Trade Commission has also warned

489
00:15:50,540 --> 00:15:53,249
about scams targeting timeshare owners who are

490
00:15:53,249 --> 00:15:54,310
trying to get out.

491
00:15:54,660 --> 00:15:56,773
Try this: if you love a resort,

492
00:15:56,773 --> 00:15:59,295
compare the full cost of a timeshare,

493
00:15:59,295 --> 00:16:00,999
including fees for years,

494
00:16:00,999 --> 00:16:03,454
with simply booking the same kind of

495
00:16:03,454 --> 00:16:04,749
vacation each year.

496
00:16:04,929 --> 00:16:06,652
And never sign on the day of

497
00:16:06,652 --> 00:16:07,576
a presentation.

498
00:16:07,756 --> 00:16:09,425
A good deal will still be a

499
00:16:09,425 --> 00:16:10,600
good deal tomorrow.

500
00:16:10,951 --> 00:16:11,996
Already in?

501
00:16:12,176 --> 00:16:14,896
Check whether your contract has a rescission

502
00:16:14,896 --> 00:16:17,370
period, a window to cancel shortly after

503
00:16:17,370 --> 00:16:17,864
signing.

504
00:16:18,044 --> 00:16:20,935
Some resorts have official exit programs.

505
00:16:21,115 --> 00:16:23,580
Be very careful with companies that promise

506
00:16:23,580 --> 00:16:24,955
to get you out for a big

507
00:16:24,955 --> 00:16:25,643
upfront fee.

508
00:16:25,823 --> 00:16:28,141
The FTC warns that some of them

509
00:16:28,141 --> 00:16:28,888
are scams.

510
00:16:29,068 --> 00:16:30,775
Start with the resort itself.

511
00:16:31,125 --> 00:16:32,460
Trap number eleven.

512
00:16:32,640 --> 00:16:34,927
Rewarding yourself because you deserve it.

513
00:16:35,277 --> 00:16:36,648
After a promotion,

514
00:16:36,648 --> 00:16:39,009
a hard year or a big milestone,

515
00:16:39,009 --> 00:16:40,989
it's natural to celebrate.

516
00:16:41,169 --> 00:16:42,005
And you should.

517
00:16:42,185 --> 00:16:43,883
The trap is when the reward is

518
00:16:43,883 --> 00:16:44,449
permanent.

519
00:16:44,629 --> 00:16:46,076
A bigger apartment,

520
00:16:46,076 --> 00:16:47,675
a luxury car payment,

521
00:16:47,675 --> 00:16:49,656
or an expensive new habit.

522
00:16:49,836 --> 00:16:51,647
These aren't one time treats.

523
00:16:51,827 --> 00:16:54,035
They're new fixed costs that follow you

524
00:16:54,035 --> 00:16:54,602
for years.

525
00:16:54,952 --> 00:16:57,509
Marketers love the phrase you deserve it,

526
00:16:57,509 --> 00:16:59,879
because it turns spending into a moral

527
00:16:59,879 --> 00:17:00,316
reward.

528
00:17:00,496 --> 00:17:02,912
But deserving something and being able to

529
00:17:02,912 --> 00:17:05,151
afford it are two different questions.

530
00:17:05,331 --> 00:17:07,727
And a permanent upgrade can quietly undo

531
00:17:07,727 --> 00:17:10,004
the progress that earned the reward in

532
00:17:10,004 --> 00:17:10,962
the first place.

533
00:17:11,312 --> 00:17:14,484
Try this: celebrate with experiences or one

534
00:17:14,484 --> 00:17:17,139
time purchases, and set a budget for

535
00:17:17,139 --> 00:17:17,361
it.

536
00:17:17,541 --> 00:17:18,456
A great dinner,

537
00:17:18,456 --> 00:17:19,372
a weekend trip,

538
00:17:19,372 --> 00:17:21,569
something you've wanted for a while.

539
00:17:21,749 --> 00:17:23,301
Then send the rest of the raise

540
00:17:23,301 --> 00:17:24,002
to your goals.

541
00:17:24,182 --> 00:17:26,270
You get the joy without resetting your

542
00:17:26,270 --> 00:17:26,654
budget.

543
00:17:27,005 --> 00:17:27,701
Already in?

544
00:17:27,881 --> 00:17:29,934
Pick the one upgrade that adds the

545
00:17:29,934 --> 00:17:31,323
least joy for its cost,

546
00:17:31,323 --> 00:17:32,711
and undo just that one.

547
00:17:32,891 --> 00:17:34,635
Maybe it's a subscription,

548
00:17:34,635 --> 00:17:37,116
a lease you can downgrade at renewal,

549
00:17:37,116 --> 00:17:39,195
or a membership you rarely use.

550
00:17:39,375 --> 00:17:41,163
You don't have to undo everything.

551
00:17:41,343 --> 00:17:43,791
One reversed upgrade can free up hundreds

552
00:17:43,791 --> 00:17:44,269
a month.

553
00:17:44,619 --> 00:17:46,024
Trap number twelve.

554
00:17:46,204 --> 00:17:48,409
Spending to match the people around you.

555
00:17:48,760 --> 00:17:51,630
When your friends move to nicer neighborhoods,

556
00:17:51,630 --> 00:17:52,753
take bigger trips,

557
00:17:52,753 --> 00:17:55,436
or send their kids to expensive activities,

558
00:17:55,436 --> 00:17:56,933
it's hard not to follow.

559
00:17:57,113 --> 00:17:59,186
It feels like normal life for your

560
00:17:59,186 --> 00:17:59,551
stage.

561
00:17:59,731 --> 00:18:01,856
But your friends might be earning more,

562
00:18:01,856 --> 00:18:03,981
inheriting money, or borrowing heavily.

563
00:18:04,161 --> 00:18:05,995
You can't see their balance sheet.

564
00:18:06,175 --> 00:18:07,545
Only their lifestyle.

565
00:18:07,895 --> 00:18:10,378
This trap is powerful because saying no

566
00:18:10,378 --> 00:18:12,224
can feel like falling behind,

567
00:18:12,224 --> 00:18:13,688
or like being left out.

568
00:18:13,868 --> 00:18:16,533
But matching someone else's spending with your

569
00:18:16,533 --> 00:18:18,386
own income is one of the fastest

570
00:18:18,386 --> 00:18:19,255
routes to debt.

571
00:18:19,435 --> 00:18:22,129
Their normal might be your financial emergency.

572
00:18:22,479 --> 00:18:24,955
Try this: find at least one friend

573
00:18:24,955 --> 00:18:26,995
who shares your money goals.

574
00:18:27,175 --> 00:18:29,520
Plan cheaper ways to spend time together.

575
00:18:29,700 --> 00:18:32,034
Hiking, potlucks, game nights.

576
00:18:32,214 --> 00:18:34,360
You'll be surprised how many people are

577
00:18:34,360 --> 00:18:36,672
quietly relieved when someone suggests it.

578
00:18:37,022 --> 00:18:37,718
Already in?

579
00:18:37,898 --> 00:18:40,754
Notice which situations trigger the spending.

580
00:18:40,934 --> 00:18:42,091
A certain group,

581
00:18:42,091 --> 00:18:43,103
a certain app,

582
00:18:43,103 --> 00:18:44,766
a certain time of year.

583
00:18:44,946 --> 00:18:46,652
You don't need to drop your friends.

584
00:18:46,832 --> 00:18:49,161
Just suggest one cheaper plan a month,

585
00:18:49,161 --> 00:18:51,611
and let your spending follow your values

586
00:18:51,611 --> 00:18:53,020
instead of the group's.

587
00:18:53,200 --> 00:18:55,197
Most real friends won't mind at all.

588
00:18:55,548 --> 00:18:56,732
Quick pause.

589
00:18:56,912 --> 00:18:58,858
If you've spotted one of your own

590
00:18:58,858 --> 00:19:00,214
decisions on this list,

591
00:19:00,214 --> 00:19:01,216
you're not alone,

592
00:19:01,216 --> 00:19:02,926
and it's not too late to fix.

593
00:19:03,106 --> 00:19:05,119
Subscribe to the channel if this is

594
00:19:05,119 --> 00:19:05,579
helping.

595
00:19:05,759 --> 00:19:07,558
It's free, and it helps me keep

596
00:19:07,558 --> 00:19:08,719
making these videos.

597
00:19:08,899 --> 00:19:10,798
Now, the traps that hit your long

598
00:19:10,798 --> 00:19:11,488
term wealth.

599
00:19:11,839 --> 00:19:12,802
Angle three.

600
00:19:12,982 --> 00:19:15,411
The traps that hit your retirement and

601
00:19:15,411 --> 00:19:16,497
long term wealth,

602
00:19:16,497 --> 00:19:18,542
and a simple filter to catch any

603
00:19:18,542 --> 00:19:20,076
trap before you step in.

604
00:19:20,426 --> 00:19:22,040
Trap number thirteen.

605
00:19:22,220 --> 00:19:24,122
Putting too much money in your own

606
00:19:24,122 --> 00:19:25,018
company's stock.

607
00:19:25,368 --> 00:19:27,585
Investing in what you know sounds smart.

608
00:19:27,765 --> 00:19:28,583
You work there,

609
00:19:28,583 --> 00:19:30,054
you believe in the company,

610
00:19:30,054 --> 00:19:31,853
and maybe you even get a discount

611
00:19:31,853 --> 00:19:32,398
on shares.

612
00:19:32,578 --> 00:19:33,820
But think about the risk.

613
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:35,350
If the company struggles,

614
00:19:35,350 --> 00:19:37,078
you could lose your job and your

615
00:19:37,078 --> 00:19:38,644
investments at the same time.

616
00:19:38,824 --> 00:19:41,301
Your paycheck and your savings both depend

617
00:19:41,301 --> 00:19:42,481
on the same company.

618
00:19:42,831 --> 00:19:45,292
The most famous example is Enron.

619
00:19:45,472 --> 00:19:48,280
When the energy company collapsed in two

620
00:19:48,280 --> 00:19:51,089
thousand one, many employees had a large

621
00:19:51,089 --> 00:19:54,037
share of their retirement savings in Enron

622
00:19:54,037 --> 00:19:57,056
stock, by some reports around sixty percent

623
00:19:57,056 --> 00:19:58,882
of the 401k plan's assets.

624
00:19:59,062 --> 00:20:00,835
They lost their jobs and much of

625
00:20:00,835 --> 00:20:02,719
their retirement at the same time.

626
00:20:02,899 --> 00:20:04,849
Loyalty was punished twice.

627
00:20:05,199 --> 00:20:08,644
Many advisors suggest keeping any single stock,

628
00:20:08,644 --> 00:20:10,550
including your employer's,

629
00:20:10,550 --> 00:20:13,115
to a small share of your portfolio,

630
00:20:13,115 --> 00:20:15,021
often ten percent or less.

631
00:20:15,201 --> 00:20:17,035
Try this: check how much of your

632
00:20:17,035 --> 00:20:19,671
total investments is in your employer's stock.

633
00:20:19,851 --> 00:20:21,215
If it's a big share,

634
00:20:21,215 --> 00:20:23,399
consider gradually diversifying,

635
00:20:23,399 --> 00:20:25,855
and ask a tax professional about the

636
00:20:25,855 --> 00:20:27,084
best way to do it.

637
00:20:27,434 --> 00:20:28,316
Already in?

638
00:20:28,496 --> 00:20:30,554
If a big share of your portfolio

639
00:20:30,554 --> 00:20:31,711
is in one company,

640
00:20:31,711 --> 00:20:34,026
you don't have to sell everything at

641
00:20:34,026 --> 00:20:34,348
once.

642
00:20:34,528 --> 00:20:36,482
Some people sell a portion on a

643
00:20:36,482 --> 00:20:38,752
schedule, for example every quarter,

644
00:20:38,752 --> 00:20:40,959
and move it into diversified funds.

645
00:20:41,139 --> 00:20:43,550
If the shares came from an employee

646
00:20:43,550 --> 00:20:45,961
plan, ask a tax professional how to

647
00:20:45,961 --> 00:20:47,339
reduce the tax bill.

648
00:20:47,689 --> 00:20:49,303
Trap number fourteen.

649
00:20:49,483 --> 00:20:52,123
Cashing out your 401k to pay off

650
00:20:52,123 --> 00:20:52,536
debt.

651
00:20:52,886 --> 00:20:54,433
When debt feels overwhelming,

652
00:20:54,433 --> 00:20:56,460
the retirement account can look like a

653
00:20:56,460 --> 00:20:56,833
rescue.

654
00:20:57,013 --> 00:20:58,313
The money is right there.

655
00:20:58,493 --> 00:21:01,029
But early withdrawals usually come with a

656
00:21:01,029 --> 00:21:01,709
heavy cost.

657
00:21:01,889 --> 00:21:04,413
Before age fifty nine and a half,

658
00:21:04,413 --> 00:21:07,625
many withdrawals are subject to income tax

659
00:21:07,625 --> 00:21:10,225
plus a ten percent additional tax,

660
00:21:10,225 --> 00:21:11,984
according to IRS rules,

661
00:21:11,984 --> 00:21:14,660
although there are some exceptions.

662
00:21:15,011 --> 00:21:18,256
That means withdrawing ten thousand dollars could

663
00:21:18,256 --> 00:21:20,639
leave you with much less after taxes

664
00:21:20,639 --> 00:21:23,752
and penalties, sometimes six or seven thousand,

665
00:21:23,752 --> 00:21:25,738
depending on your tax bracket.

666
00:21:25,918 --> 00:21:27,795
And you lose all the future growth

667
00:21:27,795 --> 00:21:29,506
that money would have produced.

668
00:21:29,686 --> 00:21:31,239
At seven percent a year,

669
00:21:31,239 --> 00:21:33,827
ten thousand dollars could grow to about

670
00:21:33,827 --> 00:21:36,350
seventy six thousand over thirty years.

671
00:21:36,700 --> 00:21:39,823
Changing jobs is another moment people cash

672
00:21:39,823 --> 00:21:40,113
out.

673
00:21:40,293 --> 00:21:43,329
Instead, many people roll the money into

674
00:21:43,329 --> 00:21:45,758
an IRA or a new employer's plan,

675
00:21:45,758 --> 00:21:48,339
which can keep the tax advantages.

676
00:21:48,519 --> 00:21:51,448
Try this: before touching retirement money,

677
00:21:51,448 --> 00:21:53,697
look at every other option first.

678
00:21:53,877 --> 00:21:56,278
Budget cuts, a debt payoff plan,

679
00:21:56,278 --> 00:21:57,928
a lower interest loan,

680
00:21:57,928 --> 00:22:00,854
a hardship program with your creditors.

681
00:22:01,205 --> 00:22:01,890
Already in?

682
00:22:02,070 --> 00:22:03,719
If you took money out early,

683
00:22:03,719 --> 00:22:04,427
don't panic.

684
00:22:04,607 --> 00:22:07,356
Restart contributions as soon as possible,

685
00:22:07,356 --> 00:22:10,040
and at least capture your employer match.

686
00:22:10,220 --> 00:22:12,286
If you took a 401k loan instead,

687
00:22:12,286 --> 00:22:14,159
keep making payments on time,

688
00:22:14,159 --> 00:22:16,613
and learn the rules about what happens

689
00:22:16,613 --> 00:22:18,033
if you leave your job,

690
00:22:18,033 --> 00:22:20,681
because some loans can become due sooner.

691
00:22:21,031 --> 00:22:23,504
Ten thousand dollars left invested at a

692
00:22:23,504 --> 00:22:26,420
seven percent average yearly return for thirty

693
00:22:26,420 --> 00:22:26,801
years.

694
00:22:26,981 --> 00:22:29,489
That's what an early withdrawal really costs.

695
00:22:29,839 --> 00:22:31,569
Trap number fifteen.

696
00:22:31,749 --> 00:22:33,992
Paying for your kids' college instead of

697
00:22:33,992 --> 00:22:35,731
saving for your own retirement.

698
00:22:36,081 --> 00:22:37,903
This one comes from love.

699
00:22:38,083 --> 00:22:40,372
Parents want to give their kids the

700
00:22:40,372 --> 00:22:42,661
best start, so they pour money into

701
00:22:42,661 --> 00:22:45,343
college savings, or pay tuition directly,

702
00:22:45,343 --> 00:22:48,416
while their own retirement savings fall behind.

703
00:22:48,596 --> 00:22:50,305
It feels like the right thing to

704
00:22:50,305 --> 00:22:50,466
do.

705
00:22:50,816 --> 00:22:53,258
But there's a hard truth financial planners

706
00:22:53,258 --> 00:22:53,997
often repeat.

707
00:22:54,177 --> 00:22:56,596
Your kids can borrow for college,

708
00:22:56,596 --> 00:22:58,282
apply for scholarships,

709
00:22:58,282 --> 00:22:59,382
work part time,

710
00:22:59,382 --> 00:23:01,874
or choose a less expensive school.

711
00:23:02,054 --> 00:23:04,330
There are no scholarships for retirement.

712
00:23:04,510 --> 00:23:06,791
If you reach sixty five without enough

713
00:23:06,791 --> 00:23:09,013
saved, the people most likely to help

714
00:23:09,013 --> 00:23:10,214
you are, ironically,

715
00:23:10,214 --> 00:23:10,814
your kids.

716
00:23:11,164 --> 00:23:12,731
This doesn't mean don't help at all.

717
00:23:12,911 --> 00:23:15,392
It means put your own oxygen mask

718
00:23:15,392 --> 00:23:16,069
on first.

719
00:23:16,249 --> 00:23:18,557
Try this: make sure you're on track

720
00:23:18,557 --> 00:23:21,590
for retirement, including your employer match,

721
00:23:21,590 --> 00:23:25,084
before contributing large amounts to college savings.

722
00:23:25,264 --> 00:23:27,244
Then help as much as you comfortably

723
00:23:27,244 --> 00:23:29,168
can, and talk openly with your kids

724
00:23:29,168 --> 00:23:31,092
about what you can and can't cover.

725
00:23:31,443 --> 00:23:32,383
Already in?

726
00:23:32,563 --> 00:23:35,865
If you've been prioritizing college over retirement,

727
00:23:35,865 --> 00:23:37,834
it's not too late to rebalance.

728
00:23:38,014 --> 00:23:40,422
Increase your retirement contributions,

729
00:23:40,422 --> 00:23:43,016
use catch up contributions if you're fifty

730
00:23:43,016 --> 00:23:45,053
or older, and have an honest talk

731
00:23:45,053 --> 00:23:47,770
with your kids about cheaper school options,

732
00:23:47,770 --> 00:23:49,067
scholarships and aid.

733
00:23:49,247 --> 00:23:51,534
Many families find a plan that works

734
00:23:51,534 --> 00:23:52,869
for both generations.

735
00:23:53,219 --> 00:23:54,478
Before the filter,

736
00:23:54,478 --> 00:23:57,138
let's talk about why smart people fall

737
00:23:57,138 --> 00:23:58,257
for these traps.

738
00:23:58,437 --> 00:24:00,109
It's not about intelligence.

739
00:24:00,289 --> 00:24:01,566
It's about persuasion.

740
00:24:01,917 --> 00:24:05,711
Psychologist Robert Cialdini spent years studying how

741
00:24:05,711 --> 00:24:07,214
people are persuaded,

742
00:24:07,214 --> 00:24:10,435
and described several principles that show up

743
00:24:10,435 --> 00:24:12,726
in almost every sales situation.

744
00:24:12,906 --> 00:24:14,334
One is social proof.

745
00:24:14,514 --> 00:24:16,182
We tend to do what people like

746
00:24:16,182 --> 00:24:16,905
us are doing.

747
00:24:17,085 --> 00:24:18,944
If everyone at work has a new

748
00:24:18,944 --> 00:24:21,058
car, or all your friends bought a

749
00:24:21,058 --> 00:24:23,173
house at the top of their budget,

750
00:24:23,173 --> 00:24:25,352
it starts to feel normal and safe.

751
00:24:25,702 --> 00:24:26,979
Another is authority.

752
00:24:27,159 --> 00:24:28,733
When a banker approves you,

753
00:24:28,733 --> 00:24:30,832
or someone with a title recommends a

754
00:24:30,832 --> 00:24:32,523
product, we tend to trust it.

755
00:24:32,703 --> 00:24:34,929
But a banker approving a loan isn't

756
00:24:34,929 --> 00:24:35,756
advising you.

757
00:24:35,936 --> 00:24:38,084
They're making a business decision for the

758
00:24:38,084 --> 00:24:38,339
bank.

759
00:24:38,519 --> 00:24:40,902
And a salesperson with an impressive title

760
00:24:40,902 --> 00:24:42,548
still gets paid when you buy.

761
00:24:42,898 --> 00:24:44,651
A third is scarcity.

762
00:24:44,831 --> 00:24:46,315
Limited time offers,

763
00:24:46,315 --> 00:24:47,354
only two left,

764
00:24:47,354 --> 00:24:48,987
this price ends today.

765
00:24:49,167 --> 00:24:51,151
When something seems scarce,

766
00:24:51,151 --> 00:24:53,347
we want it more and think less.

767
00:24:53,527 --> 00:24:56,039
That's why timeshare presentations,

768
00:24:56,039 --> 00:24:58,695
car dealers and online stores lean so

769
00:24:58,695 --> 00:25:00,202
heavily on deadlines.

770
00:25:00,382 --> 00:25:02,240
And a fourth is reciprocity.

771
00:25:02,420 --> 00:25:03,651
Accept a free gift,

772
00:25:03,651 --> 00:25:05,725
a free dinner or a free vacation

773
00:25:05,725 --> 00:25:08,058
night, and you feel a quiet pressure

774
00:25:08,058 --> 00:25:09,549
to give something back.

775
00:25:09,729 --> 00:25:10,762
Like your signature.

776
00:25:11,112 --> 00:25:13,892
Knowing these principles doesn't make you immune,

777
00:25:13,892 --> 00:25:14,630
but it helps.

778
00:25:14,810 --> 00:25:16,779
When you notice social proof,

779
00:25:16,779 --> 00:25:19,291
authority, scarcity or a free gift in

780
00:25:19,291 --> 00:25:20,445
a money decision,

781
00:25:20,445 --> 00:25:22,414
treat it like a yellow light.

782
00:25:22,594 --> 00:25:24,986
Slow down, and run the filter.

783
00:25:25,336 --> 00:25:26,620
So how do you spot a trap

784
00:25:26,620 --> 00:25:27,751
before you step in it?

785
00:25:27,931 --> 00:25:29,359
Here's a simple filter.

786
00:25:29,539 --> 00:25:31,817
Seven questions you can ask about any

787
00:25:31,817 --> 00:25:32,987
big money decision.

788
00:25:33,337 --> 00:25:34,196
Question one.

789
00:25:34,376 --> 00:25:36,245
Who benefits if I say yes?

790
00:25:36,425 --> 00:25:37,354
Follow the money.

791
00:25:37,534 --> 00:25:38,728
If a salesperson,

792
00:25:38,728 --> 00:25:41,047
a lender or a store benefits much

793
00:25:41,047 --> 00:25:42,241
more than you do,

794
00:25:42,241 --> 00:25:42,944
slow down.

795
00:25:43,124 --> 00:25:44,053
Question two.

796
00:25:44,233 --> 00:25:45,599
What's the total cost,

797
00:25:45,599 --> 00:25:46,903
not the monthly cost?

798
00:25:47,083 --> 00:25:49,881
Monthly prices hide the size of commitments.

799
00:25:50,231 --> 00:25:51,229
Question three.

800
00:25:51,409 --> 00:25:53,022
Is this a one time cost,

801
00:25:53,022 --> 00:25:54,567
or a new permanent one?

802
00:25:54,747 --> 00:25:57,359
Permanent costs deserve much more caution.

803
00:25:57,539 --> 00:25:58,480
Question four.

804
00:25:58,660 --> 00:26:00,564
What happens if things go wrong?

805
00:26:00,744 --> 00:26:01,968
If I lose my job,

806
00:26:01,968 --> 00:26:04,056
get sick or the market drops,

807
00:26:04,056 --> 00:26:06,433
does this decision make it worse?

808
00:26:06,783 --> 00:26:07,735
Question five.

809
00:26:07,915 --> 00:26:09,628
Would I make this choice if nobody

810
00:26:09,628 --> 00:26:10,283
could see it?

811
00:26:10,463 --> 00:26:13,157
This catches decisions driven by pride,

812
00:26:13,157 --> 00:26:14,608
guilt or appearances.

813
00:26:14,788 --> 00:26:15,949
Question six.

814
00:26:16,129 --> 00:26:17,441
Can I easily undo it?

815
00:26:17,621 --> 00:26:20,245
Easy to reverse decisions are low risk.

816
00:26:20,425 --> 00:26:21,846
Hard to reverse ones,

817
00:26:21,846 --> 00:26:23,945
like co signing or a timeshare,

818
00:26:23,945 --> 00:26:25,231
need extra thought.

819
00:26:25,581 --> 00:26:26,823
And question seven.

820
00:26:27,003 --> 00:26:28,980
Will I feel the same way in

821
00:26:28,980 --> 00:26:29,859
thirty days?

822
00:26:30,039 --> 00:26:32,241
Big decisions made under pressure,

823
00:26:32,241 --> 00:26:35,090
excitement or emotion often look different a

824
00:26:35,090 --> 00:26:35,867
month later.

825
00:26:36,047 --> 00:26:37,440
If it's a good decision,

826
00:26:37,440 --> 00:26:39,240
it will still be good in thirty

827
00:26:39,240 --> 00:26:39,530
days.

828
00:26:39,710 --> 00:26:42,246
If someone says the offer expires today,

829
00:26:42,246 --> 00:26:44,971
that's usually the clearest warning sign of

830
00:26:44,971 --> 00:26:45,225
all.

831
00:26:45,575 --> 00:26:46,515
Who benefits?

832
00:26:46,695 --> 00:26:47,601
Total cost?

833
00:26:47,781 --> 00:26:48,164
Permanent?

834
00:26:48,344 --> 00:26:49,528
What if it goes wrong?

835
00:26:49,708 --> 00:26:50,823
Would I do it unseen?

836
00:26:51,003 --> 00:26:51,885
Can I undo it?

837
00:26:52,065 --> 00:26:53,956
Will I feel the same in thirty

838
00:26:53,956 --> 00:26:54,271
days?

839
00:26:54,621 --> 00:26:56,887
Let's see how these traps play out

840
00:26:56,887 --> 00:26:58,220
with three examples.

841
00:26:58,400 --> 00:27:00,594
First, Nicole and James.

842
00:27:00,774 --> 00:27:02,734
They were approved for a six hundred

843
00:27:02,734 --> 00:27:03,878
thousand dollar home,

844
00:27:03,878 --> 00:27:05,511
and bought right at the limit.

845
00:27:05,691 --> 00:27:07,374
Their friends congratulated them.

846
00:27:07,554 --> 00:27:08,800
Two years later,

847
00:27:08,800 --> 00:27:09,968
a baby arrived,

848
00:27:09,968 --> 00:27:11,758
childcare costs jumped,

849
00:27:11,758 --> 00:27:13,626
and property taxes rose.

850
00:27:13,806 --> 00:27:17,047
They stopped contributing to retirement and started

851
00:27:17,047 --> 00:27:19,588
using credit cards for unexpected bills.

852
00:27:19,938 --> 00:27:21,331
Their fix wasn't dramatic.

853
00:27:21,511 --> 00:27:23,450
They refinanced when they could,

854
00:27:23,450 --> 00:27:25,449
rented out the basement for a few

855
00:27:25,449 --> 00:27:27,873
years, and rebuilt their emergency fund.

856
00:27:28,053 --> 00:27:30,693
But they lost several years of retirement

857
00:27:30,693 --> 00:27:31,594
contributions.

858
00:27:31,774 --> 00:27:33,657
Looking back, they say a house at

859
00:27:33,657 --> 00:27:36,111
eighty percent of their approval would have

860
00:27:36,111 --> 00:27:37,823
given them the same happiness,

861
00:27:37,823 --> 00:27:39,193
with none of the stress.

862
00:27:39,543 --> 00:27:40,948
Second, Robert.

863
00:27:41,128 --> 00:27:43,424
His younger brother needed a car loan

864
00:27:43,424 --> 00:27:44,728
and couldn't qualify,

865
00:27:44,728 --> 00:27:45,969
so Robert co signed.

866
00:27:46,149 --> 00:27:47,619
Eighteen months later,

867
00:27:47,619 --> 00:27:50,023
his brother lost his job and stopped

868
00:27:50,023 --> 00:27:50,491
paying.

869
00:27:50,671 --> 00:27:52,122
The lender came to Robert.

870
00:27:52,302 --> 00:27:54,545
He paid the remaining balance to protect

871
00:27:54,545 --> 00:27:56,845
his credit, but the relationship with his

872
00:27:56,845 --> 00:27:58,583
brother was strained for years.

873
00:27:58,763 --> 00:28:01,014
Robert says he'd have rather given his

874
00:28:01,014 --> 00:28:03,324
brother a few thousand dollars toward a

875
00:28:03,324 --> 00:28:04,034
cheaper car.

876
00:28:04,384 --> 00:28:06,102
And third, Diane.

877
00:28:06,282 --> 00:28:08,355
She worked at a growing company for

878
00:28:08,355 --> 00:28:10,368
fifteen years and kept most of her

879
00:28:10,368 --> 00:28:12,026
retirement in company stock,

880
00:28:12,026 --> 00:28:13,921
because the price kept going up.

881
00:28:14,101 --> 00:28:15,874
When the industry changed,

882
00:28:15,874 --> 00:28:17,442
the stock fell sharply,

883
00:28:17,442 --> 00:28:18,873
and layoffs followed.

884
00:28:19,053 --> 00:28:20,123
She kept her job,

885
00:28:20,123 --> 00:28:23,082
but her retirement savings shrank dramatically.

886
00:28:23,262 --> 00:28:24,866
She now keeps no more than a

887
00:28:24,866 --> 00:28:26,814
small share in any single company.

888
00:28:26,994 --> 00:28:29,131
Diversification isn't exciting.

889
00:28:29,311 --> 00:28:30,344
It's protection.

890
00:28:30,694 --> 00:28:32,627
Notice that none of these people were

891
00:28:32,627 --> 00:28:33,097
reckless.

892
00:28:33,277 --> 00:28:35,424
Each decision looked responsible,

893
00:28:35,424 --> 00:28:37,375
generous or loyal at the time.

894
00:28:37,555 --> 00:28:39,625
That's what makes these traps so hard

895
00:28:39,625 --> 00:28:40,017
to see.

896
00:28:40,197 --> 00:28:42,205
And that's why a simple filter,

897
00:28:42,205 --> 00:28:43,306
asked every time,

898
00:28:43,306 --> 00:28:45,897
is more powerful than willpower or luck.

899
00:28:46,247 --> 00:28:48,531
Let me answer a few questions people

900
00:28:48,531 --> 00:28:49,927
ask about these traps.

901
00:28:50,277 --> 00:28:51,043
Question one.

902
00:28:51,223 --> 00:28:52,651
Is buying a home a trap?

903
00:28:52,831 --> 00:28:53,110
No.

904
00:28:53,290 --> 00:28:55,738
Buying a home you can comfortably afford,

905
00:28:55,738 --> 00:28:57,708
with an emergency fund left over,

906
00:28:57,708 --> 00:28:59,141
can be a great decision.

907
00:28:59,321 --> 00:29:01,542
The trap is buying at the absolute

908
00:29:01,542 --> 00:29:03,044
limit of your approval,

909
00:29:03,044 --> 00:29:04,023
with no margin.

910
00:29:04,203 --> 00:29:05,736
The house is fine.

911
00:29:05,916 --> 00:29:07,402
The stretch is the problem.

912
00:29:07,752 --> 00:29:08,657
Question two.

913
00:29:08,837 --> 00:29:10,695
What if I've already co signed?

914
00:29:10,875 --> 00:29:11,839
Stay informed.

915
00:29:12,019 --> 00:29:14,425
Ask the borrower for access to the

916
00:29:14,425 --> 00:29:16,407
account, or regular updates.

917
00:29:16,587 --> 00:29:18,178
Set up alerts if you can.

918
00:29:18,358 --> 00:29:20,060
If they start to struggle,

919
00:29:20,060 --> 00:29:20,715
act early.

920
00:29:20,895 --> 00:29:22,953
Talk to them and the lender before

921
00:29:22,953 --> 00:29:24,285
late payments pile up.

922
00:29:24,465 --> 00:29:26,741
And avoid co signing again unless you'd

923
00:29:26,741 --> 00:29:28,900
be comfortable paying the whole debt.

924
00:29:29,250 --> 00:29:30,330
Question three.

925
00:29:30,510 --> 00:29:32,379
Is life insurance a trap?

926
00:29:32,559 --> 00:29:34,831
Term life insurance can be a smart,

927
00:29:34,831 --> 00:29:37,622
affordable way to protect people who depend

928
00:29:37,622 --> 00:29:38,596
on your income.

929
00:29:38,776 --> 00:29:41,192
Permanent policies, like whole life,

930
00:29:41,192 --> 00:29:43,945
are more complex and much more expensive,

931
00:29:43,945 --> 00:29:45,958
and they suit some situations,

932
00:29:45,958 --> 00:29:48,308
like certain estate planning needs.

933
00:29:48,488 --> 00:29:50,971
The trap is buying a complex policy

934
00:29:50,971 --> 00:29:54,447
mainly as an investment without understanding the

935
00:29:54,447 --> 00:29:54,873
costs.

936
00:29:55,053 --> 00:29:56,888
Ask how the agent is paid,

937
00:29:56,888 --> 00:29:57,735
and compare.

938
00:29:58,085 --> 00:29:58,979
Question four.

939
00:29:59,159 --> 00:30:00,421
How do I say no to family

940
00:30:00,421 --> 00:30:01,481
without hurting them?

941
00:30:01,661 --> 00:30:03,008
Be honest and kind.

942
00:30:03,188 --> 00:30:05,157
Something like, I love you and I

943
00:30:05,157 --> 00:30:05,958
want to help,

944
00:30:05,958 --> 00:30:07,804
but I'm not able to lend money

945
00:30:07,804 --> 00:30:09,097
or co sign right now.

946
00:30:09,277 --> 00:30:10,613
Then, if you can,

947
00:30:10,613 --> 00:30:12,735
offer another kind of help,

948
00:30:12,735 --> 00:30:14,150
like time, advice,

949
00:30:14,150 --> 00:30:15,407
or a small gift.

950
00:30:15,587 --> 00:30:18,332
A clear no often protects the relationship

951
00:30:18,332 --> 00:30:20,161
better than a reluctant yes.

952
00:30:20,512 --> 00:30:21,777
And question five.

953
00:30:21,957 --> 00:30:23,919
What's the most common trap of all?

954
00:30:24,099 --> 00:30:26,932
Probably the permanent upgrade after a raise.

955
00:30:27,112 --> 00:30:29,163
It looks completely reasonable,

956
00:30:29,163 --> 00:30:30,685
it's socially approved,

957
00:30:30,685 --> 00:30:33,266
and it quietly locks in higher spending

958
00:30:33,266 --> 00:30:33,927
for years.

959
00:30:34,107 --> 00:30:36,018
The fix is to decide where your

960
00:30:36,018 --> 00:30:38,113
next raise goes before you get it.

961
00:30:38,463 --> 00:30:39,705
Question six.

962
00:30:39,885 --> 00:30:41,607
How do I undo a trap without

963
00:30:41,607 --> 00:30:42,590
feeling ashamed?

964
00:30:42,770 --> 00:30:45,203
Remember that every trap on this list

965
00:30:45,203 --> 00:30:47,504
was designed, or socially approved,

966
00:30:47,504 --> 00:30:49,411
to look like a good decision.

967
00:30:49,591 --> 00:30:51,710
Falling for one doesn't mean you're bad

968
00:30:51,710 --> 00:30:52,308
with money.

969
00:30:52,488 --> 00:30:53,591
It means you're human.

970
00:30:53,771 --> 00:30:55,919
The skill isn't never making mistakes.

971
00:30:56,099 --> 00:30:57,567
It's noticing them early,

972
00:30:57,567 --> 00:30:59,036
fixing the next decision,

973
00:30:59,036 --> 00:31:01,150
and not letting pride keep you stuck

974
00:31:01,150 --> 00:31:02,031
in the old one.

975
00:31:02,381 --> 00:31:03,623
And question seven.

976
00:31:03,803 --> 00:31:05,463
Which trap should I fix first?

977
00:31:05,643 --> 00:31:07,304
Start with the one that costs you

978
00:31:07,304 --> 00:31:08,360
the most every month.

979
00:31:08,540 --> 00:31:09,724
For many people,

980
00:31:09,724 --> 00:31:11,501
that's housing or a car.

981
00:31:11,681 --> 00:31:14,071
For others, it's high interest debt,

982
00:31:14,071 --> 00:31:15,930
or a missing employer match.

983
00:31:16,110 --> 00:31:18,494
Fix the biggest monthly leak first,

984
00:31:18,494 --> 00:31:19,721
then the next one.

985
00:31:19,901 --> 00:31:22,176
Small fixes, stacked over a year,

986
00:31:22,176 --> 00:31:24,382
add up faster than you'd expect.

987
00:31:24,732 --> 00:31:25,556
Quick recap.

988
00:31:25,736 --> 00:31:26,707
The math traps:

989
00:31:26,707 --> 00:31:29,166
the biggest house you're approved for,

990
00:31:29,166 --> 00:31:31,690
paying off a cheap mortgage before your

991
00:31:31,690 --> 00:31:34,084
match, keeping everything in savings,

992
00:31:34,084 --> 00:31:35,896
buying because it's on sale,

993
00:31:35,896 --> 00:31:38,226
extended warranties on small things,

994
00:31:38,226 --> 00:31:39,715
and the big tax refund.

995
00:31:40,065 --> 00:31:41,515
The emotional traps:

996
00:31:41,515 --> 00:31:43,182
any degree at any cost,

997
00:31:43,182 --> 00:31:45,357
co signing, lending to family,

998
00:31:45,357 --> 00:31:48,185
the timeshare, you deserve it upgrades,

999
00:31:48,185 --> 00:31:50,142
and keeping up appearances.

1000
00:31:50,492 --> 00:31:52,274
And the long term traps:

1001
00:31:52,274 --> 00:31:54,353
loading up on company stock,

1002
00:31:54,353 --> 00:31:55,690
raiding your 401k,

1003
00:31:55,690 --> 00:31:58,735
and paying for college before retirement.

1004
00:31:58,915 --> 00:32:00,132
Plus the filter:

1005
00:32:00,132 --> 00:32:02,034
who benefits, total cost,

1006
00:32:02,034 --> 00:32:03,327
permanent or not,

1007
00:32:03,327 --> 00:32:05,000
what if it goes wrong,

1008
00:32:05,000 --> 00:32:06,598
would I do it unseen,

1009
00:32:06,598 --> 00:32:07,663
can I undo it,

1010
00:32:07,663 --> 00:32:09,717
and will I feel the same in

1011
00:32:09,717 --> 00:32:10,629
thirty days.

1012
00:32:10,979 --> 00:32:12,756
If you want the full plan for

1013
00:32:12,756 --> 00:32:15,330
avoiding traps and building wealth step by

1014
00:32:15,330 --> 00:32:16,923
step, it's in my playbook.

1015
00:32:17,103 --> 00:32:18,670
The link is in the description.

1016
00:32:18,850 --> 00:32:20,401
And if you liked this one,

1017
00:32:20,401 --> 00:32:22,547
watch my video on twenty money myths

1018
00:32:22,547 --> 00:32:23,680
that keep you poor.

1019
00:32:23,860 --> 00:32:25,764
They're close cousins of these traps.

1020
00:32:25,944 --> 00:32:27,256
I'm Ryan Rover.

1021
00:32:27,436 --> 00:32:28,562
See you in the next one.
